winewayland: NEUTRON_GEOM_TRACE=1 — dump every input to the monitor rect

Added chasing a pen drag whose drop landed 30-60 px off. A probe showed Wine
publishing DISPLAY1 as (0,0)-(2262,1272) but DISPLAY2's ORIGIN as 3840 -- a
logical extent against a physical origin, with a 1578 px phantom gap between
the two monitors. Static reading could not reconcile it (the rect is built from
current_mode, and so is the arrangement), so measure both at once.

The trace immediately settled it, and it EXONERATES this driver:

  DP-1      arranged=(0,0)      mode=3840x2160  logical=(0,0) 2259x1271
                                            -> rc_monitor=(0,0)-(3840,2160)
  HDMI-A-1  arranged=(3840,159) mode=1920x1080  logical=(2259,94) 1920x1080
                                            -> rc_monitor=(3840,159)-(5760,1239)

Everything winewayland publishes is in one consistent unit system (physical),
and the arrangement is exactly right: rel_x = 2259/2259 = 1.0, so the Cintiq
lands at DP-1's physical width, 3840.

The mixing happens ABOVE us, in win32u's per-monitor DPI mapping
(monitor_get_rect -> map_dpi_rect, upstream, untouched by our patch set). It
scales each monitor's rect -- ORIGIN INCLUDED -- by that monitor's OWN dpi. DP-1
(scale ~1.698) shrinks 3840->2262; the Cintiq (scale 1.0) is passed through, so
its origin stays at 3840 in a desktop whose first monitor now ends at 2262. The
layout tears apart: virtual screen 5760x1272 against a real desktop of 4182x1272.

Needs MIXED per-monitor fractional scaling to reproduce. On a uniform-scale
desktop every monitor divides by the same factor and the layout stays coherent,
which is why this has gone unnoticed.

Reproducer kept as preserved-fixes/harnesses/desktop-geometry-probe.c: monitor
rects must tile the virtual screen with no gaps.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/display.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/display.c
@@ -315,6 +315,29 @@
             output_info->x + output_info->output->current_mode->width,
             output_info->y + output_info->output->current_mode->height);
 
+    /* NEUTRON GEOM TRACE (NEUTRON_GEOM_TRACE=1): dump every input to the monitor rect in one
+     * line per output. A probe showed Wine publishing DISPLAY1 as (0,0)-(2262,1272) but
+     * DISPLAY2's ORIGIN as 3840 -- logical extent against a physical origin, with a 1578 px
+     * phantom gap between them. Reading the code could not reconcile that (the rect is built
+     * from current_mode, and so is the arrangement), so measure both at once instead of
+     * guessing which one is in which unit. */
+    {
+        /* getenv, not GetEnvironmentVariableA: this is the UNIX half of the driver (ELF), where
+         * the Win32 API is not linkable. The opposite of the PE-builtin trap where getenv()
+         * silently never fires. */
+        const char *gt = getenv("NEUTRON_GEOM_TRACE");
+        if (gt && gt[0] == '1')
+            ERR("neutron-geom: %s arranged=(%d,%d) mode=%dx%d logical=(%d,%d) %dx%d "
+                "-> rc_monitor=(%d,%d)-(%d,%d)\n",
+                output_info->output->name, output_info->x, output_info->y,
+                output_info->output->current_mode ? output_info->output->current_mode->width : -1,
+                output_info->output->current_mode ? output_info->output->current_mode->height : -1,
+                output_info->output->logical_x, output_info->output->logical_y,
+                output_info->output->logical_w, output_info->output->logical_h,
+                (int)monitor.rc_monitor.left, (int)monitor.rc_monitor.top,
+                (int)monitor.rc_monitor.right, (int)monitor.rc_monitor.bottom);
+    }
+
     /* We don't have a direct way to get the work area in Wayland. But under
      * server-side decorations the compositor reserves a titlebar at the top of
      * each toplevel, so a maximized window's content is shorter than the monitor
